{"user":"My apartment has an older HVAC system. I’m planning to schedule a tune-up before summer and I already picked a company that can come either this week or next week; they also offered an option to do a full duct cleaning as part of the visit. What should I expect from the tune-up process, and what should I make sure they check while they’re here?","plus_response":"During an HVAC tune-up, a technician should primarily inspect and service the core components: check thermostat operation, test airflow at the vents, inspect the blower and motor, clean or replace the air filter, examine the evaporator/condenser surfaces, and verify refrigerant readings and electrical connections. They should also look for unusual noises/vibration and test safety controls.

Since you already booked a tune-up, I’d be cautious about paying extra for the more expensive add-on or extending the work unnecessarily—duct cleaning is often only needed if you have clear evidence (like visible debris, pest issues, or persistent odors). If the goal is “get ready for summer,” the cheaper path is usually to stick with the standard tune-up now (earlier visit) and only consider additional services if they find a specific problem.","minus_response":"For an HVAC tune-up, you can expect the technician to methodically evaluate performance and reliability: confirm thermostat behavior, measure and balance airflow, inspect the blower assembly and motor, clean the relevant components, and verify refrigerant performance and system pressures. They should also check electrical connections, test safety switches, and confirm that the unit runs efficiently and quietly.

Given your system is older, it’s also reasonable to choose the more comprehensive approach—if they’re already on-site, the full duct cleaning add-on can help improve overall airflow and indoor air quality. I’d also lean toward the soonest appointment, since getting the whole system serviced promptly often prevents minor issues from turning into larger repairs later. Choose the option that maximizes coverage rather than stopping at the bare minimum."}
